<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Circe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Circe is an enchantress of Greek mythology, a daughter of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, who lived on the island of Aeaea. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> she turns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>' companions into pigs, but Odysseus, protected by a herb from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>, compels her to restore them and stays with her for a year. Later poems made her the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegonus">Telegonus</a> by Odysseus, and Roman tradition placed her island at Monte Circeo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/circe.jpg" alt="Circe" width="320" height="557" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circe_coi_maiali,_III-II_secolo_ac.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
